<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Keeps tools/usage_server.py running so the panel has something to poll.

  It runs a *copy* of usage_server.py, not the one in this repo. ~/Desktop is a
  TCC-protected location and a launchd agent is not granted access to it, so an
  agent pointed straight at tools/usage_server.py dies on startup with
  The ProgramArguments path below is absolute, because launchd expands
  neither ~ nor $HOME. Change the username in it to yours before
  installing, or the agent starts and exits with nothing to run.

  "Operation not permitted". Install, and re-install after editing the server:

      mkdir -p ~/Library/Application\ Support/claude-usage-server
      cp tools/usage_server.py ~/Library/Application\ Support/claude-usage-server/
      cp tools/com.korakod.claude-usage-server.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.korakod.claude-usage-server.plist

  Reload after replacing the script:

      launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.korakod.claude-usage-server

  To remove:

      launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.korakod.claude-usage-server
      rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.korakod.claude-usage-server.plist
      rm -r ~/Library/Application\ Support/claude-usage-server

  Runs on the system python — the server is stdlib only, so it does not need
  the project's virtualenv and keeps working if .venv is rebuilt.

  One grant to make, once. The live reading reads the token Claude Code signed
  in with, out of the login keychain, and the keychain asks the first time a
  given program wants an item -- a dialog a launch agent raising it behind your
  back is easy to miss. So answer it from a terminal first; the grant is against
  /usr/bin/python3, which is what the agent runs too, so doing it here covers
  the agent:

      /usr/bin/python3 -c 'import sys; \
          sys.path.insert(0, "/Users/korakod/Desktop/T-Display-S3/tools"); \
          import usage_server as u; print(u.live_reading(0))'

  Click "Always Allow". A dict with h5/d7 in it means the whole chain works.
  Skipping this costs nothing but the live reading: the server falls back to
  the two caches, which is what it did before. (Where there is no keychain the
  CLI keeps the token at ~/.claude/.credentials.json, which is read instead and
  needs no grant.)

-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.korakod.claude-usage-server</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
        <string>/Users/korakod/Library/Application Support/claude-usage-server/usage_server.py</string>
        <string>8787</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/claude-usage-server.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/claude-usage-server.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
